From 1762e262efa62e29429ef712c11926e0b2b8dcb9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: beat-state <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:55:11 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] lima: align the varying stride to the widest varying, not
 always to 16

lima_update_varying() rounded the PP's varying stride up to a multiple of 16 ("does component_size == 2 need to be
16 aligned?" in the comment the upstream FP16 series replaced). The Mali blob answers that question: both the RSW
field and the GP varying descriptor hold the stride in units of 8 bytes, and in the #478 capture of the Demo the
blob uses the odd multiples - varying stride 8 on every galaxy draw (one Vec4 FP16) and 24 on the horizon, chrome
and finale draws (two Vec4 FP16 and one Vec2 FP32) - where lima rounds the same layouts up to 16 and 48, writing
and reading twice the bytes it needs.

Only a varying wider than 8 bytes needs the stride to keep it aligned, and the blob agrees: every stride it uses
with a Vec4 FP32 varying is a multiple of 16 (the Demo's blur passes, 48 for 16 + 16 + 8). So align to the widest
varying, with 8 as the floor.

Bit-exact: this moves varyings in memory and changes no value. With the FP16 patch before it, lima's varying
stride matches the blob's on 52 of the 54 matched Demo draws of the capture (the other two are 16 where the blob
uses 24, lima's being the smaller), and its varying bytes per frame go from 1.46-1.96x the blob's to 0.75-1.00x
(/var/tmp/mali-re/beat-state/data/bw.json).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/gallium/drivers/lima/lima_draw.c | 10 +++++++++-
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/lima_draw.c b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/lima_draw.c
index 95447c1..a14e86e 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/lima/lima_draw.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/lima/lima_draw.c
@@ -1043,6 +1043,7 @@ lima_update_varying(struct lima_context *ctx, const struct pipe_draw_info *info,
 
    int offset = 0;
    int index = 0;
+   int max_size = 8;
 
    for (int i = 0; i < vs->state.num_outputs; i++) {
       struct lima_varying_info *v = vs->state.varying + i;
@@ -1057,9 +1058,16 @@ lima_update_varying(struct lima_context *ctx, const struct pipe_draw_info *info,
 
       v->offset = offset;
       offset += size;
+      max_size = MAX2(max_size, size);
    }
 
-   vs->state.varying_stride = align(offset, 16);
+   /* The RSW and the GP varying descriptor both hold the stride in units of 8 bytes, and the Mali blob uses the
+    * odd multiples: in the #478 capture of the Demo its galaxy draws have varying stride 8 (one Vec4 FP16) and
+    * its horizon, chrome and finale draws 24 (two Vec4 FP16 and one Vec2 FP32), where lima rounds both up to the
+    * next multiple of 16. Only a varying wider than 8 bytes (Vec4 FP32) needs the stride to keep it aligned from
+    * vertex to vertex, and the blob agrees: every stride it uses with a Vec4 FP32 varying is a multiple of 16
+    * (the Demo's blur passes, stride 48 for 16 + 16 + 8). Align to the widest varying instead of always to 16. */
+   vs->state.varying_stride = align(offset, max_size);
 
    /* gl_Position is always present, allocate space for it */
    gp_output_size = align(4 * 4 * num, 0x40);
